1. Make sure uv is installed and can be run directly.
  a. If it is installed, continue to the next step
  b. If it is not installed, offer automatic installation using AskUserQuestion
  c. If the user decides to install uv following documentation at https://raw.githubusercontent.com/astral-sh/uv/refs/heads/main/docs/getting-started/installation.md
2. Install the jira-tools CLI tool itself.  Run `uv tool install .` in the project directory.  Next make sure the tool can be run -- verify by running `jira-tools version` from the home directory.
3. Guide the user through configuration.  We need to create ~/.config/jira-tools/config.toml.
  a. If the file exists, pause and ask the user what to do
  b. The config file needs to have three entries, ask for them one by one:
    * site_url -- it's the atlassian site URL the user uses, typically it looks like http://acme.atlassian.net
    * email -- the user's email address (assigned to the Atlassian account)
    * api_token -- an access token -- instruct the user to get one at https://id.atlassian.com/manage-profile/security/api-tokens -- note that they need to chose "Create API Token", not "Create API token with scopes"
  c. With the file configured, attempt to use the tool -- run `jira-tools auth-check`
    * if it fails, offer to go back and correct settings or let the user correct them by himself
    * if it succeeds, ask for confirmation that the output is correct (the whoiam info printed by the tool)
  d. The config path is XDG-aware ($XDG_CONFIG_HOME/jira-tools/config.toml, falling back to ~/.config/jira-tools/config.toml if XDG_CONFIG_HOME is unset).
4. Run `uv run python install.py` from the clone. This installs the skill files into ~/.claude/skills and injects jira-tools usage instructions into ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md.
